11. Explain @Transactional, propagation and isolation levels.

   1. @Transactional manages database transactions and ensures operations are committed or rolled back together.
   2. Propagation defines how a method participates in an existing transaction. Common options are REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW and SUPPORTS.
   3. Isolation controls how concurrent transactions interact. Common levels are READ_UNCOMMITTED, READ_COMMITTED, REPEATABLE_READ and SERIALIZABLE.
   4. I use transactions to maintain consistency when multiple database operations must succeed or fail together.

--------------------------------------------------
12. What happens when an exception occurs inside a @Transactional method?

   1. By default, Spring rolls back the transaction for RuntimeException and Error.
   2. Checked exceptions do not trigger rollback by default.
   3. We can configure rollbackFor = Exception.class to roll back for checked exceptions as well.
   4. The transaction interceptor manages commit or rollback when the proxied method completes.

